+# Gnome workstation assignment (v0.3.81)
+
+- Each spawned standard Gnome begins without a workstation profession and performs Delver duties using a vanilla iron pickaxe.
+- Within the provisional village area (24 blocks horizontally and 12 vertically around the saved village home), a free anvil assigns one Gnome as a Smith; a free vanilla composter assigns one as a Grower.
+- Both professions are persistent and exclusive. Destroying the workstation does not change its owner or profession.
+- A Smith/Grower walks to its missing station and restores its saved block state if the space is air. If obstructed, it waits until the obstruction is removed.
+- Player- and Enderman-placed blocks within the village area are tracked. Unassigned Gnomes on Delver duty remove such foreign blocks without removing original or self-placed village blocks.
+- Every 8 ordinary Gnomes (rounded up) require an Elder. Elders do not count toward this ratio. Missing Elders are spawned, and existing Elders are never demoted or deleted when population falls.
+- Population, workstation claims, and tracked foreign block placements persist in per-dimension SavedData across unload and reload.
+- Legacy `gnome_smith` and `gnome_delver` entity IDs remain registered for save compatibility, but they now use the same workstation assignment logic as ordinary Gnomes. Their spawn eggs are still available.
+- The actual underground Gnome Village world generator and blueprint boundaries are not yet implemented. This version uses a bounded radius around each village's saved home as a temporary area.
+- Only player- and Enderman-placed blocks are currently identified as foreign; terrain/worldgen repairs and path/torch maintenance will require a structural blueprint in a later release.
